      "dom_id": "question-154270",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "What is the range of the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-6x+5?$",
      "worked_solution": "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=3>0,$ $b=-6,$ and $c=5.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{(-6)}{2\\cdot 3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=1$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3{x}^{2}-6x+5 \\\\  & =3(1)^{2}-6(1)+5 \\\\  & =3-6+5 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(1,2),$ and the range of the function is $[2,\\infty ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14269734/assets/915c1435f78f8a5f85a104b40d9ee83c54c218f8e81b6ea7fc051367055fc624.png)",
