      "dom_id": "question-250603",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{2}(x-1)^{2}-6.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$f(x)<$\n{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "The parabola is written in vertex form, \n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex. \n\n- Since $h=1$ and $k=-6,$ the vertex is $(1,-6).$\n\n- Since $a=\\frac{5}{2}>0,$ the parabola opens upwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens upwards, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nTherefore, the range is $[-6,\\infty ),$ i.e. $-6\\le f(x)<\\infty .$\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14269734/assets/365899d87325213eaf99d9ea8c2e5ac777e3617af684cdfb91b5e95fee16cb49.png)",
